1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,114
You think AI does

2
00:00:01,114 --> 00:00:02,054
maths by looking it

3
00:00:02,054 --> 00:00:02,421
up.

4
00:00:02,421 --> 00:00:03,103
Last October,

5
00:00:03,103 --> 00:00:04,176
that's all it did.

6
00:00:04,176 --> 00:00:04,630
In May,

7
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:05,797
it found something nobody

8
00:00:05,797 --> 00:00:06,699
had seen in eighty

9
00:00:06,699 --> 00:00:07,272
years.

10
00:00:07,522 --> 00:00:08,893
The puzzle is childish.

11
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:09,976
Dots on a table.

12
00:00:09,976 --> 00:00:10,956
How many pairs sit

13
00:00:10,956 --> 00:00:12,327
exactly one inch apart?

14
00:00:12,327 --> 00:00:12,986
Three dots:

15
00:00:12,986 --> 00:00:13,509
three.

16
00:00:13,509 --> 00:00:14,126
Four dots:

17
00:00:14,126 --> 00:00:14,608
five.

18
00:00:14,608 --> 00:00:15,847
Now try a million.

19
00:00:16,097 --> 00:00:16,678
In 1946,

20
00:00:16,678 --> 00:00:17,653
Erdős bet on the

21
00:00:17,653 --> 00:00:18,176
grid.

22
00:00:18,176 --> 00:00:19,258
Four hundred dots:

23
00:00:19,258 --> 00:00:20,679
one thousand seven hundred

24
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:21,247
pairs.

25
00:00:21,247 --> 00:00:22,606
Nothing beats the grid,

26
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:23,317
he said.

27
00:00:23,317 --> 00:00:24,354
For eighty years,

28
00:00:24,354 --> 00:00:25,298
nothing did.

29
00:00:25,548 --> 00:00:26,443
Then a general AI

30
00:00:26,443 --> 00:00:27,338
model built a new

31
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:28,441
family of patterns.

32
00:00:28,441 --> 00:00:29,104
No hints.

33
00:00:29,104 --> 00:00:30,293
Not built for maths.

34
00:00:30,293 --> 00:00:31,384
They climb faster than

35
00:00:31,384 --> 00:00:32,048
any grid.

36
00:00:32,048 --> 00:00:33,669
Nine mathematicians checked it.

37
00:00:33,669 --> 00:00:34,738
Erdős was wrong.

38
00:00:34,988 --> 00:00:35,729
"It's dots.

39
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:36,470
Who cares?"

40
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:36,954
Fair.

41
00:00:36,954 --> 00:00:38,311
Nobody is building anything

42
00:00:38,311 --> 00:00:39,052
with this.

43
00:00:39,052 --> 00:00:39,797
What matters:

44
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:41,031
a computer checked every

45
00:00:41,031 --> 00:00:41,515
line.

46
00:00:41,515 --> 00:00:42,583
Tens of thousands of

47
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:43,108
lines.

48
00:00:43,108 --> 00:00:44,576
It cannot be bluffed.

49
00:00:44,826 --> 00:00:45,854
Erdős left a to-do

50
00:00:45,854 --> 00:00:46,381
list.

51
00:00:46,381 --> 00:00:47,873
Twelve hundred problems.

52
00:00:47,873 --> 00:00:49,081
Six hundred and fifty

53
00:00:49,081 --> 00:00:49,933
still open.

54
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:50,609
This year,

55
00:00:50,609 --> 00:00:51,953
AI started crossing them

56
00:00:51,953 --> 00:00:52,487
off.

57
00:00:52,737 --> 00:00:54,285
So which eighty-year-old problem

58
00:00:54,285 --> 00:00:55,464
falls next?
